-Free-fall tests with the pulsar triple PSR J0337+1715 constrain body-dependent couplings with great precision, and they are usually read through static sensitivities of the bodies. The manuscript asks what free-fall timing can identify when a body has an internal state that relaxes on orbital timescales, given that the data sample the response only at a few orbital carriers and only after nuisance fitting.
+Free-fall tests with the pulsar triple PSR J0337+1715 are usually read through static sensitivities of the bodies. The manuscript asks what free-fall timing can identify when a body has an internal state that relaxes on orbital timescales, given that the data sample the response only at a few orbital carriers and after nuisance fitting.
 
 The main results are:
 \begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}\setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
 \item An exact boundary: a nonzero single pole can be matched at $K$ positive carrier frequencies by a derivative comparator of degree $N$ with unrestricted real coefficients if and only if $N\ge2K-1$.
-\item Conditions that restore identifiability, and their limits: a rate gap in a positive reciprocal spectrum gives an inequality that a slow pole violates; two calibrated complex samples identify one observable relaxation time, but finite precision cannot separate arbitrarily close poles, and a finite periodic record does not determine transients.
-\item A damped scalar-charge realization with reciprocal forces, whose leading physical drive in J0337 obeys a phase closure that an archived drive dictionary violates.
-\item A conditional application to a stored J0337 timing analysis: its interval changes by factors of 2.1--17.4 with the retained nuisance span, a calibrated six-coefficient region is validated on independent simulations, no relaxation is detected, and failed empirical-promotion gates are reported.
+\item Conditions that restore identifiability, and their limits: a rate gap in a positive reciprocal spectrum gives an inequality that a slow pole violates; two calibrated complex samples test for one observable relaxation time and recover it when a moment-variance equality holds, but finite precision cannot separate arbitrarily close poles, and a finite periodic record does not determine transients.
+\item A damped scalar-charge realization with reciprocal forces, whose leading physical drive in J0337 obeys a phase closure that the drive dictionary of our earlier stored analysis violates.
+\item A conditional application to that stored J0337 analysis: nuisance truncation had narrowed its intervals by factors of 2.1--27.5, a calibrated six-coefficient region is validated on independent simulations, and the recorded carrier coefficients show an excess that the leading physical drive, with or without relaxation, does not reproduce. No relaxation is detected, and failed empirical-promotion gates are reported.
 \end{itemize}
 
-The proofs use elementary tools; the contribution is the set of identifiability boundaries they give for this problem and their application. Material that is not needed to follow the argument is in the Supplemental Material: a finite static operator catalog, the full audits with their provenance, and a worked calculation for the inner white dwarf of the triple.
