output/submission/abstract-plain.txt:1:Proven. A linear internal state that relaxes on orbital timescales and couples to the drive and the readout adds a pole to a body's response, but a timing measurement samples that response only at a few orbital carriers and only after nuisance fitting. We state what such a measurement can identify. A nonzero single pole can be matched at K positive carrier frequencies by a derivative comparator of degree N with unrestricted real coefficients if and only if N ≥ 2K − 1. A positive reciprocal relaxation spectrum whose rates exceed a gap obeys an inequality that a slow positive pole violates. For a positive reciprocal spectrum, two exact calibrated complex samples test through a moment-variance inequality whether one observable relaxation time suffices, and recover it when equality holds; finite precision cannot uniformly separate arbitrarily close poles, and a finite periodic response record does not determine the transient response. These statements specialize classical interpolation and moment results to free-fall timing. Counterexample candidate. A damped scalar-charge model supplies reciprocal forces and a controlled one-pole reduction. Proven. Its leading physical drive in PSR J0337+1715 obeys a fixed phase closure, which the drive dictionary of our earlier stored analysis violates. Imported from prior work. In that analysis, truncating the nuisance span had narrowed the intervals on the relaxation coefficient by factors of 2.1–27.5. A calibrated six-coefficient confidence region gives 95.17–95.43 percent inclusion on independent simulations in four specified covariance conditions. The recorded carrier coefficients exceed its null threshold, but no combination of instantaneous and relaxing response to the leading physical drive at any evaluated lag reproduces them; no relaxation is detected, and the excess is not attributed. Conjectural. The result is a set of identifiability boundaries and a conditional application, not an equation-of-state-matched or drive-independent SEP bound.
